TD.add("math-stats", [

{t:"Linear Algebra", d:"The mathematics of vectors, matrices and linear transformations — the language every machine learning model is written in.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","maths"],
 b:["A neural network layer is a matrix multiplication followed by a non-linearity. An embedding is a vector. A batch of images is a tensor. PCA is an eigendecomposition. Attention is three matrix products and a softmax. Almost every operation in machine learning is linear algebra plus one non-linear step.",
    "The practical payoff is twofold: understanding why operations have the shapes they do, and recognising that GPUs exist because matrix multiplication parallelises perfectly. You need vectors, matrices, matrix multiplication, transpose, dot products, norms and eigendecomposition — not a full course."],
 k:["Matrix multiplication is the fundamental operation of deep learning.",
    "Shapes must align: (m×n)·(n×p) → (m×p). Most bugs are shape bugs.",
    "GPUs are fast because matrix multiplication parallelises perfectly.",
    "The working set is small: vectors, matrices, dot products, norms, eigenvectors."],
 dg:"vector-matrix",
 r:["Vector","Matrix","Matrix Multiplication","Eigenvalue","Tensor"]},

{t:"Vector", d:"An ordered list of numbers, representing a point or direction in space.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","maths"],
 b:["A vector has both magnitude and direction. In machine learning it is the universal representation: a data point with n features is a point in n-dimensional space, an embedding is a vector whose direction encodes meaning, and a gradient is a vector pointing uphill in parameter space.",
    "The operations that matter are addition (combining), scaling, dot product (measuring alignment) and norm (measuring length). Cosine similarity between embeddings is just a normalised dot product."],
 k:["Represents a point or a direction in n-dimensional space.",
    "A data row, an embedding and a gradient are all vectors.",
    "Dot product measures alignment; norm measures length.",
    "Normalising to unit length makes dot product equal cosine similarity."],
 dg:"vector-matrix",
 r:["Matrix","Dot Product","Norm","Embedding","Cosine Similarity"]},

{t:"Matrix", d:"A rectangular grid of numbers representing a linear transformation or a table of data.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","maths"],
 b:["A matrix has two readings that are both useful. As data, each row is an observation and each column a feature. As a transformation, multiplying a vector by a matrix rotates, scales, shears or projects it — which is exactly what a neural network layer does to its input.",
    "Shape notation (rows × columns) governs everything. Reading a shape mismatch error is the most common debugging task in machine learning, and it is always about which dimension needs to align."],
 k:["Two readings: a data table, or a linear transformation.",
    "Shape is (rows × columns) and determines what can multiply what.",
    "A neural network layer applies a weight matrix to its input.",
    "Transpose swaps rows and columns and appears constantly in gradients."],
 dg:"vector-matrix",
 r:["Vector","Matrix Multiplication","Tensor","Linear Algebra"]},

{t:"Matrix Multiplication", d:"Combining two matrices by taking dot products of rows with columns — the core computation of neural networks.", l:"core", g:["operation","maths"],
 b:["Element (i, j) of the product is the dot product of row i of the first matrix with column j of the second. This requires the inner dimensions to match: (m×n)·(n×p) gives (m×p). It is not commutative — AB and BA are generally different matrices, and often one is not even defined.",
    "Every forward pass through a dense layer, every attention computation and every convolution (which reduces to matrix multiplication after im2col) is this operation. GPU tensor cores exist specifically to do it faster."],
 k:["Inner dimensions must match; the outer dimensions give the result shape.",
    "Not commutative: AB ≠ BA in general.",
    "Cost is O(m·n·p) — this is where training time goes.",
    "Batched matrix multiplication is what GPUs are optimised for."],
 x:{lang:"python", code:'import numpy as np\n\nX = np.random.randn(32, 784)     # batch of 32 flattened images\nW = np.random.randn(784, 256)    # layer weights\nb = np.zeros(256)\n\nH = X @ W + b                     # (32, 784) @ (784, 256) -> (32, 256)\nprint(H.shape)'},
 dg:"vector-matrix",
 r:["Matrix","Dot Product","Neural Network","Linear Algebra","GPU"]},

{t:"Dot Product", d:"Multiplying two vectors elementwise and summing the results, giving a measure of how aligned they are.", l:"core", g:["operation","maths"],
 b:["The dot product equals |a||b|cos θ, so it is large and positive when vectors point the same way, zero when they are perpendicular, and negative when opposed. That is why it measures similarity between embeddings and why attention scores are dot products between queries and keys.",
    "For unit vectors the dot product is exactly the cosine of the angle between them — which is why vector databases normalise embeddings and then use the cheaper dot product instead of computing cosine similarity."],
 k:["Equals |a||b|cos θ — a direct measure of alignment.",
    "Zero means orthogonal; negative means pointing apart.",
    "Attention scores are dot products of queries and keys.",
    "On unit vectors it equals cosine similarity exactly."],
 r:["Vector","Cosine Similarity","Attention Mechanism","Norm"]},

{t:"Norm", d:"A measure of a vector's length or magnitude.", l:"intermediate", g:["operation","maths"],
 b:["The L2 (Euclidean) norm is the square root of the sum of squares — ordinary geometric length. The L1 (Manhattan) norm is the sum of absolute values. The L∞ norm is the largest absolute component.",
    "Norms appear throughout machine learning: as regularisation penalties (L1 and L2), in gradient clipping (rescale when the gradient norm is too large), and in normalising embeddings to unit length before similarity search."],
 k:["L2 is Euclidean length; L1 is the sum of absolute values.",
    "L1 regularisation produces sparsity; L2 produces smooth shrinkage.",
    "Gradient clipping rescales by the global gradient norm.",
    "Dividing a vector by its norm makes it unit length."],
 r:["Vector","L1 and L2 Regularisation","Gradient Clipping","Euclidean Distance"]},

{t:"Eigenvalue", a:"Eigenvalue and Eigenvector", d:"A vector whose direction is unchanged by a transformation, and the factor by which it is scaled.", l:"advanced", g:["fundamentals","maths"],
 b:["For a matrix A, an eigenvector v satisfies Av = λv: applying the transformation only stretches or shrinks v, never rotates it. λ is the eigenvalue, the scaling factor. Eigenvectors are the natural axes of a transformation.",
    "This is why PCA works: the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix are the directions of greatest variance, and their eigenvalues say how much variance each direction carries. Eigenvalues also govern stability in dynamical systems and PageRank in graphs."],
 k:["Av = λv — direction preserved, magnitude scaled.",
    "PCA takes the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix as its components.",
    "Eigenvalues rank the components by explained variance.",
    "SVD generalises the idea to non-square matrices."],
 dg:"pca",
 r:["Principal Component Analysis","Singular Value Decomposition","Matrix","Linear Algebra"]},

{t:"Singular Value Decomposition", a:"SVD", d:"Factorising any matrix into a rotation, a scaling and another rotation.", l:"advanced", g:["operation","maths"],
 b:["SVD writes any matrix A as UΣVᵀ, where U and V are orthogonal and Σ is diagonal with non-negative singular values. Unlike eigendecomposition it applies to any matrix, including rectangular ones.",
    "Keeping only the largest k singular values gives the best possible rank-k approximation, which underlies PCA, latent semantic analysis, matrix-factorisation recommenders, image compression and low-rank adaptation methods like LoRA."],
 k:["A = UΣVᵀ, and it exists for every matrix.",
    "Truncating to the top k singular values gives the optimal low-rank approximation.",
    "Underlies PCA, LSA and matrix-factorisation recommenders.",
    "The rank intuition behind LoRA comes from here."],
 r:["Eigenvalue","Principal Component Analysis","Matrix","LoRA"]},

{t:"Derivative", d:"The instantaneous rate of change of a function — how much the output moves when the input moves a little.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","calculus"],
 b:["Geometrically the derivative is the slope of the tangent line. In optimisation it tells you which way to move a parameter to reduce the loss and how sensitive the loss is to that parameter. A derivative of zero marks a minimum, maximum or saddle point.",
    "Everything in gradient-based learning follows from this: you compute how the loss changes with respect to each weight and step in the opposite direction."],
 k:["Slope of the function at a point.",
    "Zero derivative means a stationary point — minimum, maximum or saddle.",
    "Sign says which direction reduces the function; magnitude says how steeply.",
    "The foundation of all gradient-based optimisation."],
 r:["Gradient","Partial Derivative","Chain Rule","Gradient Descent"]},

{t:"Partial Derivative", d:"The derivative with respect to one variable, holding all the others fixed.", l:"intermediate", g:["fundamentals","calculus"],
 b:["A loss function depends on millions of parameters. The partial derivative ∂L/∂w tells you how the loss changes if you nudge only w, keeping everything else constant. Collecting all partial derivatives into a vector gives the gradient.",
    "This is what backpropagation computes: the partial derivative of the loss with respect to every single weight, efficiently, by reusing intermediate results through the chain rule."],
 k:["Vary one input, hold the rest constant.",
    "The gradient is the vector of all partial derivatives.",
    "Backpropagation computes one per weight.",
    "Written ∂L/∂w rather than dL/dw to signal partiality."],
 r:["Derivative","Gradient","Chain Rule","Backpropagation"]},

{t:"Gradient", d:"The vector of all partial derivatives, pointing in the direction of steepest increase.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","calculus"],
 b:["The gradient ∇L collects ∂L/∂w for every parameter. It points uphill, in the direction that increases the loss fastest, which is why gradient descent steps in the opposite direction. Its magnitude indicates how steep the surface is.",
    "A vanishing gradient means the parameter has almost no influence and learning stalls; an exploding gradient means one step will overshoot catastrophically. Both are diagnosed by monitoring gradient norms during training."],
 k:["Points in the direction of steepest increase — so step against it.",
    "One component per parameter; the gradient has the same shape as the weights.",
    "Magnitude near zero means learning has stalled for that parameter.",
    "Monitoring the gradient norm is a standard training diagnostic."],
 dg:"gradient-descent",
 r:["Partial Derivative","Gradient Descent","Backpropagation","Vanishing Gradient"]},

{t:"Chain Rule", d:"The rule for differentiating nested functions: multiply the derivatives of each layer of nesting.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","calculus"],
 b:["If y = f(g(x)), then dy/dx = f'(g(x)) · g'(x). For a deep network, the loss is a deeply nested composition of layers, and the chain rule lets you compute the derivative with respect to any early weight by multiplying derivatives along the path back from the loss.",
    "This is exactly what backpropagation implements. It also explains vanishing and exploding gradients directly: multiplying many factors below one shrinks toward zero, and many factors above one grows without bound."],
 k:["Derivatives of nested functions multiply along the chain.",
    "Backpropagation is the chain rule applied efficiently in reverse.",
    "Many small factors multiplied → vanishing gradient.",
    "Many large factors multiplied → exploding gradient."],
 dg:"backprop",
 r:["Backpropagation","Gradient","Derivative","Vanishing Gradient"]},

{t:"Convex Optimisation", d:"Optimising a function shaped like a bowl, where any local minimum is guaranteed to be the global minimum.", l:"advanced", g:["optimisation","maths"],
 b:["A convex function has no local minima other than the global one, so gradient descent cannot get stuck. Linear regression, logistic regression, SVMs and lasso all have convex objectives, which is why they train reliably and reproducibly.",
    "Neural network losses are decidedly non-convex, with many local minima and vastly more saddle points. In practice this matters far less than early theory feared: in very high dimensions, most local minima turn out to have similar loss values."],
 k:["Any local minimum of a convex function is the global minimum.",
    "Linear and logistic regression, SVMs and lasso are convex.",
    "Neural networks are non-convex — training is not guaranteed to find the best solution.",
    "In high dimensions, saddle points outnumber local minima."],
 dg:"gradient-descent",
 r:["Gradient Descent","Loss Function","Linear Regression","Cost Function"]},

{t:"Probability", d:"A number between 0 and 1 expressing how likely an event is.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["Probability underpins classification (predicting P(class|features)), uncertainty quantification, sampling and the entire framework of statistical inference. The two interpretations — frequentist (long-run frequency) and Bayesian (degree of belief) — lead to different methodologies but the same arithmetic.",
    "The axioms are minimal: probabilities lie in [0,1], the total over all outcomes is 1, and the probability of mutually exclusive events is the sum of theirs. Almost everything else follows."],
 k:["Range [0, 1]; probabilities of all outcomes sum to 1.",
    "Frequentist and Bayesian are interpretations, not different mathematics.",
    "Independent events: P(A and B) = P(A)·P(B).",
    "Classification models output probabilities, not decisions."],
 r:["Conditional Probability","Bayes Theorem","Probability Distribution","Random Variable"]},

{t:"Conditional Probability", d:"The probability of one event given that another has occurred.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B) — restrict attention to the cases where B happened, and ask how often A also happened. Machine learning classification is entirely conditional probability: P(spam | these words).",
    "The most common error is confusing P(A|B) with P(B|A). The probability of a positive test given disease is not the probability of disease given a positive test, and for rare conditions those two numbers differ enormously — the base rate fallacy."],
 k:["P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B) — restrict the sample space to B.",
    "P(A|B) ≠ P(B|A); confusing them is the base rate fallacy.",
    "Independence means P(A|B) = P(A).",
    "Every classifier estimates a conditional probability."],
 dg:"bayes",
 r:["Bayes Theorem","Probability","Naive Bayes"]},

{t:"Bayes Theorem", d:"A rule for updating a belief when new evidence arrives.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["P(A|B) = P(B|A)·P(A) / P(B). The prior P(A) is what you believed before, the likelihood P(B|A) is how well the evidence fits that hypothesis, and the posterior P(A|B) is your updated belief.",
    "The canonical demonstration: a test 99% accurate for a disease affecting 1 in 10,000 people. A positive result still means only about 1% chance of having the disease, because the prior is so low. Ignoring the prior is the single most common probabilistic mistake in practice."],
 k:["Posterior ∝ likelihood × prior.",
    "The base rate dominates when a condition is rare.",
    "Underlies Naive Bayes, Bayesian inference and A/B test analysis.",
    "Prior beliefs are made explicit, which is a feature not a flaw."],
 dg:"bayes",
 r:["Conditional Probability","Naive Bayes","Probability"]},

{t:"Random Variable", d:"A quantity whose value is determined by the outcome of a random process.", l:"intermediate", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["A random variable maps outcomes to numbers: the result of a die roll, tomorrow's demand, whether a user clicks. Discrete random variables take countable values and have a probability mass function; continuous ones take any value in a range and have a probability density function.",
    "Every column in a dataset is a realisation of a random variable, which is why distributions, expectations and variances are the natural tools for describing data."],
 k:["Maps random outcomes to numbers.",
    "Discrete variables have a mass function; continuous ones a density.",
    "For continuous variables, P(X = exact value) = 0 — only intervals have probability.",
    "Each data column is a sample from some random variable."],
 r:["Probability Distribution","Expected Value","Variance","Probability"]},

{t:"Probability Distribution", d:"A description of how probability is spread across the possible values of a random variable.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["A distribution says which values are likely and which are not. The common ones each model a characteristic situation: normal for measurements affected by many small factors, binomial for counts of successes, Poisson for rare events in a fixed interval, exponential for waiting times, uniform for equally likely outcomes.",
    "Choosing the right distribution encodes an assumption about the data-generating process, and that assumption drives which statistical test or model is appropriate."],
 k:["Discrete distributions use a mass function; continuous use a density.",
    "Total probability always integrates or sums to 1.",
    "Normal, binomial, Poisson, exponential and uniform cover most cases.",
    "Plot your data before assuming a distribution."],
 dg:"normal-dist",
 r:["Normal Distribution","Random Variable","Expected Value","Central Limit Theorem"]},

{t:"Normal Distribution", a:"Gaussian Distribution", d:"The symmetric bell-shaped distribution defined by its mean and standard deviation.", l:"core", g:["distribution","statistics"],
 b:["The normal distribution appears everywhere because of the central limit theorem: any quantity that is the sum of many small independent effects tends toward it. Heights, measurement errors and test scores are approximately normal for that reason.",
    "The 68-95-99.7 rule is worth memorising: about 68% of values lie within one standard deviation of the mean, 95% within two and 99.7% within three. Many statistical tests assume normality, so check it before relying on them."],
 k:["Fully described by mean μ and standard deviation σ.",
    "68-95-99.7 within one, two and three standard deviations.",
    "Arises from summing many small independent effects.",
    "Real data with heavy tails or skew is not normal — verify before assuming."],
 dg:"normal-dist",
 r:["Central Limit Theorem","Standard Deviation","Probability Distribution","Z-Score"]},

{t:"Binomial Distribution", d:"The distribution of the number of successes in a fixed number of independent yes/no trials.", l:"intermediate", g:["distribution","statistics"],
 b:["Flip a coin 100 times and count heads; send 1,000 emails and count opens; run 500 trials and count conversions. Parameters are n (number of trials) and p (probability of success), giving mean np and variance np(1−p).",
    "It is the foundation of conversion-rate analysis and A/B testing. For large n it is well approximated by a normal distribution, which is why normal-based confidence intervals work for proportions."],
 k:["Fixed n independent trials, each with probability p of success.",
    "Mean = np, variance = np(1−p).",
    "The basis of conversion rate and click-through analysis.",
    "Approximates a normal distribution for large n."],
 r:["Probability Distribution","Normal Distribution","A/B Testing"]},

{t:"Poisson Distribution", d:"The distribution of how many rare, independent events occur in a fixed interval.", l:"intermediate", g:["distribution","statistics"],
 b:["Requests per second, defects per batch, support tickets per hour, radioactive decays per minute. It has a single parameter λ, the average rate, and unusually its mean and variance are both equal to λ.",
    "It models arrivals, which makes it the natural distribution for queueing theory, capacity planning and anomaly detection on count data. When observed variance substantially exceeds the mean, the data is overdispersed and a negative binomial fits better."],
 k:["One parameter λ, which is both the mean and the variance.",
    "Models counts of independent events in a fixed interval.",
    "Underlies queueing theory and capacity planning.",
    "Variance far above the mean signals overdispersion — use negative binomial."],
 r:["Probability Distribution","Binomial Distribution","Expected Value","Anomaly Detection"]},

{t:"Expected Value", a:"Mean", d:"The long-run average of a random variable, weighting each outcome by its probability.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["E[X] = Σ x·P(x). It is the centre of mass of a distribution and the value you would converge to by averaging infinitely many samples. Expected value calculations underpin decision-making under uncertainty: an option worth £1,000 with probability 0.1 has an expected value of £100.",
    "It can be misleading when the distribution is skewed or heavy-tailed. The expected value of a lottery ticket is negative; the expected outcome of a startup investment is dominated by rare enormous successes."],
 k:["Probability-weighted average of all outcomes.",
    "Linear: E[aX + bY] = aE[X] + bE[Y], regardless of dependence.",
    "Misleading for skewed distributions — check the median too.",
    "The basis of decision-making under uncertainty."],
 r:["Variance","Mean, Median and Mode","Random Variable","Probability Distribution"]},

{t:"Variance", d:"The average squared distance of values from their mean — a measure of spread.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["Variance quantifies how spread out a distribution is. Squaring makes all deviations positive and weights large deviations heavily, which is mathematically convenient but leaves the result in squared units. Standard deviation, its square root, restores the original units.",
    "In machine learning, variance also names a specific failure mode: a high-variance model is one whose predictions swing dramatically with small changes in the training data."],
 k:["Average of squared deviations from the mean.",
    "Squared units — take the square root for interpretability.",
    "Sample variance divides by n−1, not n, to correct bias.",
    "High model variance means sensitivity to the training sample — overfitting."],
 r:["Standard Deviation","Expected Value","Bias-Variance Trade-off","Covariance"]},

{t:"Standard Deviation", d:"The square root of variance — spread expressed in the same units as the data.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["Standard deviation is the everyday measure of spread because it is interpretable: if average delivery time is 3 days with a standard deviation of 1 day, most deliveries take 2 to 4 days. For normal data the 68-95-99.7 rule turns it directly into probability statements.",
    "It is sensitive to outliers, since it inherits variance's squaring. When outliers are legitimate, the interquartile range or median absolute deviation describe spread more robustly."],
 k:["Same units as the data, unlike variance.",
    "For normal data: 68% within 1σ, 95% within 2σ.",
    "Sensitive to outliers — consider IQR or MAD when they are present.",
    "Standardisation subtracts the mean and divides by this."],
 dg:"normal-dist",
 r:["Variance","Normal Distribution","Z-Score","Feature Scaling"]},

{t:"Covariance", d:"A measure of whether two variables move together.", l:"intermediate", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["Positive covariance means the two variables tend to be above or below their means at the same time; negative means one is high when the other is low; near zero means no linear relationship. Its magnitude depends on the variables' units, so it cannot be compared across pairs.",
    "The covariance matrix — every pairwise covariance — is central to PCA, portfolio optimisation and multivariate normal modelling. Correlation is covariance normalised to a comparable scale."],
 k:["Sign shows the direction of the linear relationship.",
    "Magnitude is unit-dependent and therefore not comparable across pairs.",
    "The covariance matrix drives PCA and portfolio theory.",
    "Zero covariance does not imply independence — only no linear relationship."],
 r:["Correlation","Variance","Principal Component Analysis"]},

{t:"Correlation", d:"A normalised measure of linear relationship between two variables, ranging from −1 to 1.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["Pearson correlation divides covariance by the two standard deviations, producing a unitless number: 1 is a perfect increasing linear relationship, −1 perfect decreasing, 0 no linear relationship. Spearman correlation does the same on ranks, capturing any monotonic relationship.",
    "Two cautions dominate practice. Correlation only detects linear relationships — a perfect parabola has correlation near zero. And correlation is not causation: the relationship may run the other way, or both may be driven by a third variable."],
 k:["Range −1 to 1, unitless and comparable across variable pairs.",
    "Pearson measures linear; Spearman measures monotonic via ranks.",
    "Near-zero correlation does not mean no relationship — plot the data.",
    "Correlation never establishes causation on its own."],
 r:["Covariance","Correlation vs Causation","Feature Selection","Confounding Variable"]},

{t:"Mean, Median and Mode", d:"Three measures of a distribution's centre, each behaving differently under skew.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["The mean is the arithmetic average and is pulled by extreme values. The median is the middle value and is robust to them. The mode is the most frequent value and is the only one meaningful for categorical data.",
    "The gap between mean and median is a quick skewness check. UK household income has a mean well above its median because a small number of very high incomes drag the average up — which is why median income is the number normally reported."],
 k:["Mean is sensitive to outliers; median is robust.",
    "Mean above median indicates right skew, and vice versa.",
    "Mode is the only centre measure valid for categorical data.",
    "Report the median for skewed distributions such as income or latency."],
 r:["Expected Value","Skewness","Outlier","Percentile"]},

{t:"Percentile", a:"Quantile", d:"The value below which a given percentage of observations fall.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["The 95th percentile is the value that 95% of observations are below. Quartiles split data into four equal parts; the interquartile range (Q3 − Q1) covers the middle half and is a robust measure of spread.",
    "Percentiles are how latency is properly reported. Mean response time hides the tail; p95 and p99 describe what your slowest users actually experience, and those are the numbers that determine whether a service feels fast."],
 k:["p95 means 95% of values fall below it.",
    "IQR = Q3 − Q1 is a robust spread measure, immune to outliers.",
    "Latency must be reported as percentiles, never as a mean.",
    "Percentiles do not average — you cannot combine p99s across servers by averaging."],
 r:["Mean, Median and Mode","Outlier","Latency","Standard Deviation"]},

{t:"Outlier", d:"A data point far from the rest of the distribution.", l:"core", g:["data","statistics"],
 b:["Outliers are either errors (a sensor glitch, a data entry mistake, a test transaction) or genuine rare events (a fraud case, a viral post, an equipment failure). Which one determines what you should do, and you cannot tell from the number alone.",
    "Common detection rules are the 1.5×IQR fence and z-scores beyond 3, though both assume roughly symmetric data. Deleting outliers without investigating them is how the interesting cases — often exactly what you are trying to predict — get thrown away."],
 k:["Investigate before removing — an outlier may be the signal.",
    "1.5×IQR and |z| > 3 are the usual detection heuristics.",
    "Tree models are robust to outliers; linear models and k-means are not.",
    "Winsorising caps extremes without discarding the row."],
 r:["Percentile","Anomaly Detection","Standard Deviation","Data Quality"]},

{t:"Skewness", d:"A measure of how asymmetric a distribution is.", l:"intermediate", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["Right (positive) skew has a long tail toward high values — income, latency, file sizes, city populations. Left skew has a long tail toward low values, which is rarer in practice. Symmetric distributions have zero skew.",
    "Skew matters because many models and tests assume roughly symmetric data. A log transform often makes right-skewed data approximately normal, which improves linear model behaviour considerably."],
 k:["Right skew: mean above median, long upper tail.",
    "Latency, income and file sizes are almost always right skewed.",
    "A log transform frequently symmetrises right-skewed data.",
    "Affects the validity of tests and models that assume normality."],
 r:["Normal Distribution","Mean, Median and Mode","Outlier","Feature Engineering"]},

{t:"Central Limit Theorem", a:"CLT", d:"The result that the average of many independent samples is approximately normally distributed, whatever the original distribution.", l:"intermediate", g:["theory","statistics"],
 b:["Take repeated samples from any distribution — uniform, exponential, wildly skewed — compute each sample's mean, and those means will follow a normal distribution centred on the true mean, with standard deviation σ/√n. Roughly 30 samples is usually enough.",
    "This is why so much of statistics works: confidence intervals, t-tests and A/B test analysis all rely on the sampling distribution of the mean being normal, without requiring the underlying data to be."],
 k:["Sample means become normal regardless of the population's distribution.",
    "Standard error is σ/√n — halving it requires four times the data.",
    "Around n = 30 is the usual rule of thumb.",
    "Justifies confidence intervals and t-tests on non-normal data."],
 dg:"normal-dist",
 r:["Normal Distribution","Confidence Interval","Hypothesis Testing","Sampling"]},

{t:"Sampling", d:"Selecting a subset of a population to draw conclusions about the whole.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["Random sampling gives every member an equal chance and is what makes inference valid. Stratified sampling ensures each subgroup is represented proportionally. Cluster sampling selects whole groups, which is cheaper but less precise.",
    "Sampling bias is the dominant real-world risk: survey respondents differ from non-respondents, users who complete onboarding differ from those who abandon it, and a model trained on the survivors learns the wrong thing. The 1936 Literary Digest poll remains the textbook example."],
 k:["Random selection is what makes generalisation valid.",
    "Stratified sampling preserves subgroup proportions.",
    "Sampling bias invalidates conclusions regardless of sample size.",
    "Survivorship bias is the most common and least visible form."],
 r:["Central Limit Theorem","Bias","Training Data","Confidence Interval"]},

{t:"Hypothesis Testing", d:"A procedure for deciding whether observed data provides enough evidence against a default assumption.", l:"core", g:["inference","statistics"],
 b:["State a null hypothesis (no effect), choose a significance level α (usually 0.05), compute a test statistic and its p-value, and reject the null if p < α. The logic is proof by contradiction under uncertainty: if the data would be very unlikely under the null, doubt the null.",
    "Failing to reject is not evidence of no effect — it may simply mean the sample was too small. And testing many hypotheses without correction guarantees false positives: at α = 0.05, twenty independent tests produce one spurious significant result on average."],
 k:["Set α before looking at the data, not after.",
    "Failing to reject the null is not evidence that the null is true.",
    "Multiple comparisons need correction (Bonferroni, Benjamini-Hochberg).",
    "Statistical significance is not practical importance."],
 r:["Null Hypothesis","P-Value","Statistical Significance","Type I and Type II Error","A/B Testing"]},

{t:"Null Hypothesis", d:"The default assumption that there is no effect or no difference, which a test attempts to reject.", l:"core", g:["inference","statistics"],
 b:["The null hypothesis states the boring outcome: this drug has no effect, this variant performs no differently, these groups have the same mean. The alternative hypothesis is what you suspect. The test asks whether the data is sufficiently improbable under the null to abandon it.",
    "The asymmetry is deliberate and important: you can accumulate evidence against the null, but never prove it true. Absence of evidence for an effect is not evidence of its absence."],
 k:["States no effect, no difference, no relationship.",
    "You reject or fail to reject — you never accept it.",
    "Frames the test as proof by contradiction under uncertainty.",
    "Decide the null and the alternative before collecting data."],
 r:["Hypothesis Testing","P-Value","Statistical Significance","Type I and Type II Error"]},

{t:"P-Value", d:"The probability of seeing data at least this extreme if the null hypothesis were true.", l:"core", g:["inference","statistics"],
 b:["A p-value of 0.03 means that if there were genuinely no effect, data this extreme would occur 3% of the time. It is emphatically not the probability that the null hypothesis is true, nor the probability that your result is a fluke, nor a measure of effect size.",
    "P-hacking — trying many analyses, subgroups or stopping points until something crosses 0.05 — is the mechanism behind a large share of unreplicable published findings. Pre-registering the analysis plan is the defence."],
 k:["P(data this extreme | null true) — not P(null true | data).",
    "Says nothing about how large or important the effect is.",
    "The 0.05 threshold is a convention, not a law of nature.",
    "Report effect size and confidence interval alongside it, always."],
 r:["Hypothesis Testing","Statistical Significance","Confidence Interval","Null Hypothesis"]},

{t:"Confidence Interval", d:"A range of plausible values for a parameter, with a stated long-run coverage rate.", l:"core", g:["inference","statistics"],
 b:["A 95% confidence interval means that if you repeated the study many times, 95% of the intervals constructed this way would contain the true value. It is a statement about the procedure, not about this particular interval.",
    "Confidence intervals are more informative than p-values because they show both the direction and the plausible magnitude of an effect. An interval of [0.1%, 0.3%] and one of [0.1%, 40%] may both exclude zero, but they support very different decisions."],
 k:["95% refers to the long-run coverage of the procedure, not this interval.",
    "Width reflects precision — narrow intervals mean more information.",
    "An interval excluding zero corresponds to significance at that level.",
    "More decision-useful than a p-value because it shows magnitude."],
 r:["P-Value","Hypothesis Testing","Central Limit Theorem","Statistical Significance"]},

{t:"Statistical Significance", d:"The judgement that an observed effect is unlikely to be due to chance alone.", l:"core", g:["inference","statistics"],
 b:["An effect is called statistically significant when its p-value falls below the chosen threshold. That is a statement about chance, not about importance: with a large enough sample, a conversion rate difference of 0.001% becomes significant while remaining commercially irrelevant.",
    "The useful discipline is to decide, before running a test, what effect size would actually change your decision — the minimum detectable effect — and to power the test to detect that."],
 k:["Significant means unlikely under chance, not important.",
    "Large samples make trivial differences significant.",
    "Define the minimum effect that would change your decision, in advance.",
    "Report effect size and interval, not just the significance verdict."],
 r:["P-Value","Hypothesis Testing","A/B Testing","Statistical Power"]},

{t:"Type I and Type II Error", d:"The two ways a hypothesis test can be wrong: a false positive and a false negative.", l:"core", g:["inference","statistics"],
 b:["A type I error rejects a true null — you conclude there is an effect when there is none. Its rate is α, the significance level you chose. A type II error fails to reject a false null — you miss a real effect. Its rate is β, and 1 − β is the test's power.",
    "The two trade off: making α stricter to avoid false alarms increases the chance of missing real effects. Which matters more is a domain decision — a false positive in drug approval and a false negative in cancer screening carry very different costs."],
 k:["Type I = false positive, rate α. Type II = false negative, rate β.",
    "Power = 1 − β, conventionally targeted at 0.8.",
    "Tightening α raises the type II rate unless you increase sample size.",
    "The relative cost of each error is a domain judgement."],
 dg:"confusion-matrix",
 r:["Hypothesis Testing","Statistical Power","P-Value","Confusion Matrix"]},

{t:"Statistical Power", d:"The probability that a test will detect an effect of a given size, if that effect is real.", l:"intermediate", g:["inference","statistics"],
 b:["Power is 1 − β, conventionally targeted at 80%. It depends on the effect size you want to detect, the sample size, the variability of the data and your chosen α. Power analysis run before an experiment tells you how much data you need.",
    "Underpowered studies are doubly bad: they miss real effects, and the effects they do find are systematically overestimated, because only unusually large observed effects clear the significance bar in a small sample."],
 k:["Power = 1 − β; 0.80 is the usual target.",
    "Calculate the required sample size before running the test.",
    "Underpowered studies exaggerate the effects they do detect.",
    "Reducing variance raises power as effectively as adding samples."],
 r:["Type I and Type II Error","Hypothesis Testing","A/B Testing","Sampling"]},

{t:"Maximum Likelihood Estimation", a:"MLE", d:"Choosing the parameter values that make the observed data most probable.", l:"advanced", g:["inference","statistics"],
 b:["Given a model family and some data, MLE asks which parameter values would have made this data most likely to occur, and picks those. In practice you maximise the log-likelihood, since sums are easier to differentiate than products and numerically better behaved.",
    "Most standard estimators are maximum likelihood estimators in disguise: minimising squared error is MLE under Gaussian noise, and minimising cross-entropy is MLE for a categorical model. Recognising that unifies a lot of apparently separate methods."],
 k:["Pick the parameters that maximise P(observed data | parameters).",
    "Log-likelihood is maximised for numerical convenience.",
    "Least squares is MLE under Gaussian noise assumptions.",
    "Cross-entropy minimisation is MLE for classification."],
 r:["Loss Function","Cross-Entropy","Probability","Bayes Theorem"]},

{t:"Entropy", d:"A measure of uncertainty in a probability distribution — how surprising its outcomes are on average.", l:"intermediate", g:["information-theory","maths"],
 b:["Entropy is highest when all outcomes are equally likely and zero when one outcome is certain. A fair coin has 1 bit of entropy; a coin that always lands heads has none. It quantifies the average information content of a random variable.",
    "In machine learning it appears as the impurity measure in decision trees (information gain is the entropy reduction from a split) and as the basis of cross-entropy loss."],
 k:["Maximum when outcomes are equally likely; zero when one is certain.",
    "Measured in bits with base-2 logarithms, nats with natural logs.",
    "Decision trees split to maximise entropy reduction.",
    "Cross-entropy loss derives directly from it."],
 r:["Cross-Entropy","KL Divergence","Information Gain","Decision Tree"]},

{t:"KL Divergence", a:"Kullback-Leibler Divergence", d:"A measure of how much one probability distribution differs from another.", l:"advanced", g:["information-theory","maths"],
 b:["KL divergence measures the extra information needed to encode samples from distribution P using a code optimised for Q. It is zero when the distributions are identical and grows as they diverge. Crucially it is asymmetric — KL(P‖Q) ≠ KL(Q‖P) — so it is a divergence, not a distance.",
    "It appears as the regularisation term in variational autoencoders, as the constraint keeping RLHF from drifting too far from the base model, and as a drift detection statistic comparing production feature distributions to training ones."],
 k:["Zero when identical; grows with difference.",
    "Asymmetric — not a true distance metric.",
    "Constrains policy drift in RLHF and regularises VAE latent spaces.",
    "Used to detect data drift between training and production distributions."],
 r:["Entropy","Cross-Entropy","Variational Autoencoder","Data Drift","RLHF"]},

{t:"Information Gain", d:"The reduction in entropy achieved by splitting data on a particular feature.", l:"intermediate", g:["information-theory","algorithm"],
 b:["A decision tree evaluates candidate splits by how much they reduce uncertainty about the label. Information gain is entropy before the split minus the weighted average entropy after it; the split with the highest gain is chosen.",
    "It has a bias toward features with many distinct values, since splitting on a unique identifier perfectly separates the training data. Gain ratio normalises for this, and Gini impurity is the computationally cheaper alternative most implementations use by default."],
 k:["Entropy before the split minus weighted entropy after.",
    "Biased toward high-cardinality features — gain ratio corrects it.",
    "Gini impurity is cheaper and gives near-identical trees.",
    "The splitting criterion in ID3, C4.5 and their descendants."],
 r:["Entropy","Decision Tree","Gini Impurity","Feature Selection"]},

{t:"Gini Impurity", d:"The probability that a randomly chosen element would be misclassified by a random label drawn from the node's distribution.", l:"intermediate", g:["algorithm","statistics"],
 b:["Gini impurity is zero when a node contains a single class and highest when classes are evenly mixed. Decision trees choose the split that most reduces it, weighted by the size of the resulting nodes.",
    "It requires no logarithms, so it is faster to compute than entropy, and in practice produces nearly identical trees. It is the default criterion in scikit-learn's tree implementations."],
 k:["Zero for a pure node; maximum for an evenly mixed one.",
    "Cheaper than entropy — no logarithms required.",
    "Produces trees almost indistinguishable from information gain.",
    "Distinct from the Gini coefficient used in economics."],
 r:["Decision Tree","Information Gain","Entropy","Random Forest"]},

{t:"Euclidean Distance", d:"The straight-line distance between two points, computed by the Pythagorean theorem generalised to n dimensions.", l:"core", g:["metric","maths"],
 b:["The square root of the sum of squared coordinate differences. It is the default distance in k-means, KNN and many clustering algorithms, and corresponds to the L2 norm of the difference between two vectors.",
    "It requires comparable scales — an unscaled feature measured in thousands dominates one measured in fractions — and it degrades in high dimensions, where all points become roughly equidistant. For text embeddings, cosine similarity is generally preferred."],
 k:["√Σ(aᵢ − bᵢ)² — the L2 norm of the difference.",
    "Requires feature scaling to be meaningful.",
    "Degrades in high dimensions as distances converge.",
    "Cosine similarity is usually better for text embeddings."],
 r:["Cosine Similarity","Norm","K-Means Clustering","Curse of Dimensionality"]},

{t:"Z-Score", a:"Standard Score", d:"How many standard deviations a value lies from the mean.", l:"core", g:["metric","statistics"],
 b:["z = (x − μ)/σ. It puts values from different distributions onto a common scale, so a z-score of 2 means the same thing whether the underlying variable is height or income. Standardisation is exactly this transformation applied to a whole feature.",
    "For normally distributed data, |z| > 2 covers roughly the outer 5% and |z| > 3 the outer 0.3%, which is why 3 is a common outlier threshold. It is only meaningful for roughly symmetric distributions."],
 k:["Number of standard deviations from the mean.",
    "Puts different variables on a comparable scale.",
    "|z| > 3 is a common outlier heuristic for symmetric data.",
    "Standardisation applies this transformation feature by feature."],
 r:["Standard Deviation","Normal Distribution","Feature Scaling","Outlier"]},

{t:"Correlation vs Causation", d:"The distinction between two variables moving together and one of them causing the other.", l:"core", g:["reasoning","statistics"],
 b:["Two variables can correlate because A causes B, because B causes A, because a third variable C causes both, because of selection effects in how the data was collected, or by pure coincidence in a large search space. Correlation alone cannot distinguish these.",
    "Establishing causation requires a randomised experiment, or a quasi-experimental design that credibly approximates one — instrumental variables, difference-in-differences, regression discontinuity. Machine learning models learn correlation, which is why acting on a model's feature importances as if they were causal effects is a serious error."],
 k:["Reverse causation, confounding, selection and coincidence all produce correlation.",
    "Randomised experiments are the reliable route to causal claims.",
    "ML models learn association; they do not identify causal effects.",
    "Feature importance is not an intervention effect."],
 r:["Correlation","Confounding Variable","A/B Testing","Feature Importance"]},

{t:"Confounding Variable", d:"A third variable that influences both the supposed cause and the supposed effect, creating a spurious association.", l:"intermediate", g:["reasoning","statistics"],
 b:["Ice cream sales correlate with drowning deaths; the confounder is summer, which drives both. In observational data, plausible confounders are everywhere, and controlling for the ones you thought of does not rule out the ones you did not.",
    "Randomisation solves the problem structurally: assigning treatment at random means confounders are balanced across groups in expectation, whether or not you know what they are."],
 k:["Causes both the exposure and the outcome, creating a false link.",
    "Statistical adjustment only handles confounders you measured.",
    "Randomisation balances unknown confounders automatically.",
    "Simpson's paradox is confounding at its most dramatic."],
 r:["Correlation vs Causation","Simpson's Paradox","A/B Testing","Bias"]},

{t:"Simpson's Paradox", d:"When a trend visible in every subgroup reverses once the groups are combined.", l:"advanced", g:["reasoning","statistics"],
 b:["The classic case is the 1973 Berkeley admissions data: women were admitted at a lower rate overall, yet at a higher rate in most individual departments. The explanation is that women applied disproportionately to departments with low admission rates for everyone.",
    "The lesson is that aggregate statistics can invert the truth. Always segment by the variables that plausibly matter before drawing conclusions — and be aware that this is precisely why A/B tests must be analysed on the randomised units, not on post-hoc groupings."],
 k:["Subgroup trends can reverse entirely on aggregation.",
    "Caused by unequal group sizes combined with a confounder.",
    "Always inspect segmented results, not only the total.",
    "A structural reason to prefer randomised experiments."],
 r:["Confounding Variable","Correlation vs Causation","A/B Testing","Bias"]},

{t:"Bootstrapping", d:"Estimating uncertainty by repeatedly resampling your data with replacement.", l:"advanced", g:["technique","statistics"],
 b:["Draw a sample of the same size as your dataset, with replacement, compute the statistic of interest, and repeat a thousand times. The spread of those values estimates the sampling distribution of the statistic — without any assumption about the underlying distribution.",
    "It is invaluable when analytical standard errors are unavailable or the distributional assumptions are dubious, which covers most complex statistics. It is also the resampling mechanism inside bagging and random forests."],
 k:["Resample with replacement, recompute, repeat — typically 1,000+ times.",
    "Needs no distributional assumptions.",
    "The 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles give a 95% confidence interval.",
    "The same mechanism underlies bagging and random forests."],
 r:["Confidence Interval","Bagging","Sampling","Random Forest"]},

{t:"Monte Carlo Method", d:"Solving a problem by running many random simulations and averaging the results.", l:"advanced", g:["technique","maths"],
 b:["When a quantity is hard to compute analytically, simulate the process many times and take the empirical average. Estimating π by throwing random points at a square, valuing an option by simulating price paths, or estimating project completion time by sampling task durations all work this way.",
    "Accuracy improves as 1/√n, so a tenfold accuracy improvement requires a hundredfold more samples. Monte Carlo tree search applies the same idea to game playing and underpins AlphaGo-style systems."],
 k:["Simulate many times and average the outcomes.",
    "Error shrinks as 1/√n — accuracy is expensive.",
    "Handles problems with no closed-form solution.",
    "MCTS applies it to sequential decision-making."],
 r:["Sampling","Probability","Bootstrapping","Reinforcement Learning"]},

{t:"Markov Chain", d:"A process where the next state depends only on the current state, not on the history that led there.", l:"advanced", g:["theory","maths"],
 b:["The Markov property — memorylessness — makes a system tractable: a transition matrix of probabilities from each state to each other state fully describes it. Over time many chains converge to a stationary distribution independent of where they started.",
    "PageRank is the stationary distribution of a random walk on the web graph. Markov chains also underpin MCMC sampling, hidden Markov models for speech and bioinformatics, and the state formalism of reinforcement learning."],
 k:["The next state depends only on the current state.",
    "A transition matrix fully specifies the process.",
    "Many chains converge to a stationary distribution regardless of the start.",
    "PageRank, MCMC and hidden Markov models all rest on this."],
 r:["Markov Decision Process","Probability","Reinforcement Learning","Monte Carlo Method"]},

{t:"Markov Decision Process", a:"MDP", d:"The formal framework of reinforcement learning: states, actions, transition probabilities and rewards.", l:"advanced", g:["theory","reinforcement"],
 b:["An MDP is defined by a set of states, a set of actions, transition probabilities P(s'|s,a), a reward function R(s,a) and a discount factor γ that weights future reward against immediate reward. Solving it means finding a policy — a mapping from states to actions — that maximises expected discounted return.",
    "It assumes the Markov property holds, which real problems frequently violate. When the agent cannot observe the full state, the correct formalism is a partially observable MDP, which is considerably harder."],
 k:["Defined by states, actions, transitions, rewards and a discount factor γ.",
    "The solution is a policy mapping states to actions.",
    "γ near 0 is myopic; γ near 1 weights the long term heavily.",
    "Partial observability makes it a POMDP and much harder to solve."],
 r:["Reinforcement Learning","Markov Chain"]},

{t:"Statistics", d:"The discipline of collecting, describing and drawing conclusions from data under uncertainty.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","statistics"],
 b:["Descriptive statistics summarise what the data shows — means, spreads, distributions, plots. Inferential statistics reason from a sample to a population, quantifying how confident that reasoning can be. Machine learning grew out of the second, with more emphasis on prediction than on explanation.",
    "The practical divide is that statistics traditionally asks what is true and how sure can we be, while machine learning asks what will happen next. Both need the same foundations, and the most common failures in both are about the data rather than the method."],
 k:["Descriptive summarises; inferential generalises to a population.",
    "Statistics emphasises explanation; ML emphasises prediction.",
    "Sampling and measurement problems invalidate any downstream method.",
    "Always plot the data before computing anything."],
 r:["Probability","Hypothesis Testing","Machine Learning","Sampling","Data Visualisation"]},

{t:"Data Visualisation", d:"Presenting data graphically so that patterns, trends and anomalies become visible.", l:"core", g:["communication","analysis"],
 b:["Anscombe's quartet — four datasets with identical means, variances and correlations but wildly different shapes — is the standard argument for plotting before summarising. Distribution plots, scatter plots and time series reveal what summary statistics conceal.",
    "The design rules that matter: match the chart type to the question (trend to line, comparison to bar, proportion to a small pie or stacked bar), start bar chart axes at zero, avoid dual axes, never rely on colour alone, and label directly rather than forcing readers to consult a legend."],
 k:["Anscombe's quartet: identical statistics, completely different data.",
    "Trend → line, comparison → bar, distribution → histogram or box plot.",
    "Bar chart axes must start at zero; line charts need not.",
    "Colour alone must never carry meaning — accessibility requires redundancy."],
 r:["Statistics","Exploratory Data Analysis","R","Percentile"]},

{t:"Exploratory Data Analysis", a:"EDA", d:"Investigating a dataset with summaries and plots before modelling it.", l:"core", g:["workflow","analysis"],
 b:["EDA is where you learn what you actually have: distributions and their skew, missing value patterns, correlations, outliers, class balance, duplicates, and whether the data matches what the documentation claims. It routinely surfaces problems that would otherwise be discovered after a week of modelling.",
    "Look specifically for leakage candidates — features that are suspiciously predictive — and for missingness that is not random, since a field that is missing only for one group carries information about that group."],
 k:["Distributions, missingness, correlations, outliers, duplicates, class balance.",
    "Suspiciously predictive features usually indicate leakage.",
    "Missingness is often informative rather than random.",
    "Time spent here saves far more time later."],
 r:["Data Visualisation","Data Quality","Feature Engineering","Data Leakage"]}

]);
